# LINE ENDINGS — full consolidation on LF. Operator decision, 2026-08-19.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# LF is the only style compatible with all three platforms. Windows tools,
# Python, git, VS Code and PowerShell all read LF without complaint. A stray
# carriage return is NOT ignored on Mac or Linux: it becomes part of the line's
# content, so `#!/bin/sh<CR>` makes the system look for an interpreter literally
# named `sh<CR>` and the failure reads "bad interpreter" without ever naming the
# real cause.
#
# WHAT THIS CHANGES: NOTHING IN STORAGE.
#
# Measured 2026-08-19 across all 670 tracked text files: every blob was ALREADY
# LF -- 667 LF, 3 with no newline at all, zero CRLF, zero mixed. The repository
# was already consolidated; `core.autocrlf=true` normalised on the way in. This
# file changes only what CHECKOUT writes to disk, so it produces no diff noise
# and no blob moves by a byte.
#
# THE LIVE DEFECT IT CLOSES
#
# `autocrlf=true` converts on the way OUT. Measured in a fresh worktree, which
# is a real checkout: `run_acervator.sh`, `build_mac.sh` and
# `deploy/kiosk/install.sh` all arrived CRLF. So every fresh clone on Windows
# got broken shell scripts. This
# working tree escaped only because it was never checked out -- it is the
# original folder that was `git init`-ed, so its files kept whatever endings
# they already had.
#
# AND IT PROTECTS THE GATE ITSELF
#
# `.githooks/pre-push` is a shell script. On a fresh Windows clone it would have
# arrived CRLF, failed with "bad interpreter", and the pre-push gate would have
# stopped running SILENTLY -- the enforcement mechanism disabling itself on
# exactly the machines that need it. The `.githooks` rule below is not
# decoration.
#
# VERIFY, rather than trusting this comment:
#   git ls-files --eol            # i/lf w/lf for every text file
#   git add --renormalize . && git status --porcelain   # must be empty

# Everything text: normalise in the index, hand out LF on checkout, everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Executable scripts, stated explicitly. A CR here does not produce a diff --
# it produces a non-functioning program, so this must not depend on `text=auto`
# guessing right.
*.sh        text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# NO SOURCE FILE IS PINNED TO CRLF ANY MORE. The rule above is the whole rule.
#
# `src/trading/scrumming_bot.py` was pinned here to `eol=crlf`, because
# `tests/test_autonomous_fold_price_gate.py` read it as BYTES, split its lines
# on CRLF, and pinned a sha256 of the exact CRLF bytes. Without the pin a fresh
# checkout handed that test LF, its gate anchors found nothing, and 80 of its
# 122 tests failed with StalePlant. So one file in the repository was stored
# LF and written to disk CRLF, to satisfy one reader.
#
# 2026-08-19: that reader was repaired instead. It now normalises once at read
# time and matches LINES rather than a byte layout, and its pre-change digest
# is taken over the NORMALISED text, so the same content answers the same in
# either form. Its own control asserts that: the reconstruction from the CRLF
# rendering must equal the reconstruction from the LF one.
#
# So the pin is not merely deleted. What it protected is now checked, in the
# test, in both directions -- which the pin never did.

# Binaries: never touched, never normalised, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.so   binary
*.dylib binary
*.pyd  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.qm   binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
